Fixed. The stragglers were:

```
Running Simplification (EPSILON: 0.5, DELTA: 200)...
SIMPLIFY_CORE_MS: 0.0000
Simplified to 0 points (nan%).
```

**Root cause:** `parse_arguments` returned `0` after printing help (both the `-h` and "no args" branches), but `main` ignored that and kept going into `read_stream` → `simplify()` on an empty stream, producing those three lines.

**Fix:** Added a `help_flag` in `simplify_io.h` (set in both help paths) and a short-circuit in `simplify.cpp` so `main` returns immediately after help is shown:

```40:43:simplify_io.h
inline bool out_flag = false;
inline bool dist_flag = false;
inline bool web_server_flag = false;
inline bool help_flag = false;
```

```95:101:simplify_io.h
if (argc == 1) {
    print_help(argv[0]);
    help_flag = true;
    return 0;
}
```

```300:306:simplify.cpp
std::vector<Point> stream;
if (help_flag) {
    return 0;
}
code = read_stream(test_case_no, argv, stream);
```

Verified:
- `./simplify -h` and `./simplify` now print only the help text (exit 0).
- `./simplify 10` still runs end-to-end and emits the full `Input file / Output file / Loaded N / Running Simplification / SIMPLIFY_CORE_MS / Simplified to ...` output.
